From: Manoj Kumar <manojkumar.oct14@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH v5] blk-wbt: Do not treat active reads as pure-write state when write samples are sparse

When write completions in a stat window do not meet RWB_MIN_WRITE_SAMPLES,
stat_sample_valid() returns false. Currently, latency_exceeded() classifies
any window with write activity (samples, in-flight, or recent wait) as
LAT_UNKNOWN_WRITES without checking if read samples are present.

In wb_timer_fn(), LAT_UNKNOWN_WRITES unconditionally calls scale_up(),
which decrements scale_step to negative values and boosts write queue
depth. While this boost is intended when only writes are occurring, doing
so in mixed workloads where reads are active and write completions are
sparse (< 3 in a 100ms window) causes write queue depth to expand
despite concurrent read traffic.

Ensure LAT_UNKNOWN_WRITES is only returned when there are truly no read
samples in the window (!stat[READ].nr_samples). When read samples are
present but write samples are insufficient, return LAT_UNKNOWN so the
controller preserves the status quo rather than expanding write depth.

Signed-off-by: Manoj Kumar <manojkumar.oct14@gmail.com>
---
 block/blk-wbt.c | 3 ++-
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/block/blk-wbt.c b/block/blk-wbt.c
--- a/block/blk-wbt.c
+++ b/block/blk-wbt.c
@@ -330,7 +330,8 @@ static int latency_exceeded(struct rq_wb *rwb, struct blk_rq_stat *stat)
 	if (!stat_sample_valid(stat)) {
 		/*
 		 * If we had writes in this stat window and the window is
 		 * current, we're only doing writes. If a task recently
 		 * waited or still has writes in flights, consider us doing
 		 * just writes as well.
 		 */
-		if (stat[WRITE].nr_samples || wb_recent_wait(rwb) ||
+		if (!stat[READ].nr_samples &&
+		    (stat[WRITE].nr_samples || wb_recent_wait(rwb) ||
 		     wbt_inflight(rwb)))
 			return LAT_UNKNOWN_WRITES;
 		return LAT_UNKNOWN;
